#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151863 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "e49f7799-2724-5fe0-999f-bfa9ab5ce610" :question/math-academy-id "e-6244" :question/problem "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {0, 1, 2}, P_{2} = {1, 2, 3}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P)$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/94f8ae5652f9bf448a84bd30a23dbe09.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + M_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + M_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$. We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum,\n\nTaking into account the fact that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})=1\n$$\n\nfor all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11} + M_{21} + M_{12} + M_{22}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/c32290b459f231155ebed12f7dbb2413.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/e53b15ce6facb291ce49ed2b43c131db.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each top right corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{11} &= f(1, 2) = 5 \\\\\nM_{21} &= f(2, 2) = 6 \\\\\nM_{12} &= f(1, 3) = 7 \\\\\nM_{22} &= f(2, 3) = 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $U$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= 5 + 6 + 7 + 8 = 26\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151864 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "1ac785c8-3d14-5968-94d3-6f4588f08d91" :question/math-academy-id "e-6245" :question/problem "Let $R = [1,5] \\times [2,6]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {1, 3, 5}, P_{2} = {2, 4, 6}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P)$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/9b2abc9b7e3a6bfcb6d7041a24fce9aa.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + M_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + M_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$. We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})=4\n$$\n\nfor all $4$ regions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= 4 \\cdot M_{11} + 4 \\cdot M_{21} + 4 \\cdot M_{12} + 4 \\cdot M_{22} \\\\\n&= 4(M_{11} + M_{21} + M_{12} + M_{22})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/143b73cdc2be16101dddbb200c3c39b6.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/09e5f4950e59ffc259e3ed3bef452a2f.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each bottom left corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{11} &= f(1, 2) = 60 \\\\\nM_{21} &= f(3, 2) = 52 \\\\\nM_{12} &= f(1, 4) = 48 \\\\\nM_{22} &= f(3, 4) = 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $U$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= 4(60 + 52 + 48 + 40) = 800\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151865 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa8da8f-00bb-5663-b578-99086398a1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Upper Bound in Terms of Maximum Values"}] :question/id #uuid "14e77998-b24b-56c1-8901-dc94d3ef98a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6246" :question/problem "Consider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R$. Find an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/7bd8e396e842ee10967b4656ad76cdd5.png)\n\n*Hint: $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $2$ rectangular solids, one for each $R_{ij}$.\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion $R_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_{ij}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}$. We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU = M_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{21})\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) = 8\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU &= 8 \\cdot M_{11} + 4 \\cdot M_{21} \\\\\n&= 8M_{11} + 4M_{21}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa8da8f-00bb-5663-b578-99086398a1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Upper Bound in Terms of Maximum Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97d415b0-6c5c-53d2-9d2c-4d6dd0353cd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M_{11} \\cdot M_{21} \\cdot M_{12} \\cdot M_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148855} {:answer/id #uuid "a1cb6ea2-bdd6-547b-a4bb-ede86c15e21f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(M_{11} + M_{21} + M_{12} + M_{22})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148856} {:answer/id #uuid "ac617ab9-1584-56b9-ab51-0541d60e2c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M_{11} - M_{21} + M_{12} - M_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148857} {:answer/id #uuid "cdc96657-5863-5b52-bf69-d9a6de137de7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M_{11} + M_{21} + M_{12} + M_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148858} {:answer/id #uuid "de8afcbf-c1bb-537e-9566-e29997041eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2M_{11} + M_{21} + 2M_{12} + M_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de8afcbf-c1bb-537e-9566-e29997041eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2M_{11} + M_{21} + 2M_{12} + M_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148859} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef0062db-958f-5d6c-a60a-f5c94523fcb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148854}] :question/id #uuid "3bab7c56-0d70-56a8-b7e8-20549b73d742" :question/math-academy-id "q-122847" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/q-122847.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x, y)$ defined on $R$. Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}$*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89071eb7-32aa-5054-ac9a-bf45b50dc4ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150715} {:answer/id #uuid "f08f2d03-41d0-5cbc-8927-6c288db88a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150716} {:answer/id #uuid "102be2cf-56dd-50c0-bbe4-ce9976bb3983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150717} {:answer/id #uuid "32addc48-c7e6-5b0c-94a9-359b235268ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150718} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd61a8b-ad3a-54c0-b6ca-d88b60500700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f08f2d03-41d0-5cbc-8927-6c288db88a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150716} :answer-field/id #uuid "57d73b5f-0ee0-53d1-baf6-217b604bd60f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150714}] :question/id #uuid "06416db6-a5ef-5504-be7c-78a29bd00e0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75048" :question/problem "Let $R = [- 1, 1] \\cdot [- 1, 1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = 5 - 3x - y$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {-1, 0, 1}, P_{2} = {-1, 0, 1}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f, P)$."}]}
